# Response headers for static hosts that support this file.
#
# Cloudflare Pages and Netlify both read `_headers` from the root of the publish
# directory, so this file is inert on GitHub Pages (which serves it as an
# ordinary file and cannot set headers) and takes effect the moment the site is
# deployed to either of those. No code change is needed at that point.
#
# X-Frame-Options is kept alongside the CSP `frame-ancestors` directive, which a
# <meta http-equiv> policy cannot enforce. Both are deliberate: the header covers
# older browsers, frame-ancestors covers the modern ones.

/*
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), geolocation=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31556952; includeSubDomains

# Content-Security-Policy is deliberately not repeated here. It is currently
# delivered by the <meta http-equiv> tag in static/src/app/layout.tsx, which every
# host honours. When the site moves off GitHub Pages, move that policy into this
# file and delete the meta tag in the same commit. Do not leave both: a response
# header overrides the meta tag, so a stale copy here would silently undo any
# tightening done in the component.
#
# The `preload` directive is also omitted on purpose. It is not something a file
# can opt into: a domain must be submitted to the browser preload list by hand,
# and removal is effectively irreversible. On github.io it is not available at
# all, since that is a shared domain. Treat it as a decision for a custom domain
# you control.